      "latency_ms": 2753.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 667,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to understand water is to understand the very fabric of life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The States of Water\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard, flowing state found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F), found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils or evaporates, present in the air as humidity and in clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unusual scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports nutrients in our bodies and minerals in the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats on water, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, which allows aquatic life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite how abundant it looks, about **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiology considers water to be the cradle of life. \n*   Life is believed to have originated in Earth's oceans billions of years ago.\n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It cushions our brains, lubricates our joints, regulates our temperature, and flushes out waste. \n*   No known living organism—from bacteria to blue whales to humans—can survive without water.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with miraculous properties that make it the driving force of chemistry, weather, geology, and life itself."
